{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 Helvetica;}}\f0\fs24 {\b Topology}\par 1. Skim - MODIFY: highlighted the credit agreement (add-back cap, cost savings window, cash netting limit, leverage step-down) and then the compliance certificate where it doesn't match\par 2. Google Sheets - MODIFY: reviewed each add-back for eligibility and recalculated EBITDA, net debt and both covenant ratios\par 3. Skim - LOOKUP: went back to the equity cure section to check the cure conditions and the prior cure\par 4. Google Sheets - MODIFY: sized the minimum cure, checked the pro forma ratio and downloaded the workbook as .xlsx\par 5. Pages - CREATE: wrote the credit memo to my portfolio manager and exported it as a PDF\par \par {\b Request}\par I'm a leveraged finance associate at the agent bank on a sponsor-backed term loan, and the borrower just sent in its Q3 2026 compliance certificate. The leverage covenant steps down to 5.25x this quarter and they're reporting 5.13x, which is close enough that I want to rebuild the numbers myself instead of taking theirs at face value. I'll go through the credit agreement in Skim and mark up the EBITDA add-back cap, the cash netting limit and the equity cure section, then mark up the certificate wherever it doesn't line up with those definitions. In Google Sheets I'll check each add-back, apply the caps, recalculate leverage and interest coverage, and work out the minimum equity cure if they turn out to be out of compliance. Then I'll write a short credit memo in Pages for my portfolio manager with what I found and what I think we should do next. Deliverables are the marked-up PDFs, the covenant workbook and the memo as a PDF.\par \par {\b Task Description}\par 1. In Skim, read the EBITDA and net debt definitions and the covenant table, and highlight the 25% add-back cap (measured before add-backs), the 18-month cost savings window, the $15M cash netting limit and the 5.25x step-down.\par 2. In Skim, highlight where the borrower's certificate goes off: the cap is worked off EBITDA after add-backs, the Dayton savings don't qualify, and they netted all $28M of cash.\par 3. In Google Sheets, check the LTM numbers against the certificate and mark each add-back eligible or not against the 18-month cutoff.\par 4. In Google Sheets, apply the 25% cap and the cash netting limit, then recalculate leverage and interest coverage and compare against the borrower's numbers.\par 5. Check the cure conditions in Skim, then in Google Sheets size the smallest cure that fixes it, confirm the pro forma ratio and download the workbook.\par 6. In Pages, write the credit memo with what I found, the covenant results and my recommendation, and export it as a PDF.\par }